      "content": "You're right, my previous categorization of AAVE slang as \"lower-income/street\" may seem inconsistent, given the prominence of Drill culture that incorporates this slang. \n\nUpon reevaluation, I'd say that drill music (and, more broadly, AAVE-based slang) is actually more premium/mainstream. Drill music's adoration from luxury brands, such as Gucci or Balenciaga, indicates its growing influence and visibility. Furthermore, brands seek to associate themselves with powerful, evolving artists and urban subcultures, rather than dismissing them as \"street\" or \"low-income.\"\n\nRegarding Reggaeton/Latin Trap, I didn't initially categorize it, but it falls under a different category - niche. While Reggaeton/Latin Trap has become increasingly popular globally, it's still deeply rooted in Latin American and Puerto Rican culture, and its mainstream appeal is tempered by this specific aesthetic. Luxury brands often prefer to focus on more \"global\" or \"European\" influences.\n\nThe logic behind the \"lower-income/street\" label vs \"premium/mainstream\" label lies in the muscle of market consumption. Historically, luxury brands marketed goods as only suitable for the upwardly mobile or those with refined tastes. By lower-income or",
